(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-12"] = {"12.1":{"s":"Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids all contain the carbonyl group, C=O. In an aldehyde the carbonyl carbon carries at least one hydrogen (the –CHO group); in a ketone it is joined to two alkyl or aryl groups. A carboxylic acid has an –OH on the carbonyl carbon, the –COOH group, which makes it behave differently from the other two."},"12.2":{"s":"Each family is split by the carbon skeleton on the carbonyl group. Aliphatic aldehydes have –CHO on an sp³ carbon (formaldehyde counts as aliphatic too); aromatic ones, like benzaldehyde, have it on a benzene ring. Ketones are simple (both groups the same) or mixed, and aliphatic or aromatic. Carboxylic acids are aliphatic (including the long-chain fatty acids) or aromatic, and are also grouped by the number of –COOH groups.","w":["Phenylacetaldehyde (C₆H₅CH₂CHO) is aliphatic: the –CHO is not on the ring."]},"12.3":{"s":"Common names come from the related acid (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde) or name the two groups on a ketone (dimethyl ketone). In IUPAC names the ending -e of the alkane becomes -al for aldehydes, -one for ketones and -oic acid for acids. The chain is numbered so the carbonyl or carboxyl carbon gets the lowest number; in aldehydes and acids that carbon is always C-1.","w":["In an aldehyde or acid the functional carbon is C-1, so no locant is written: butanal, not butan-1-al."]},"12.4":{"s":"Aldehydes and ketones are made by oxidising or dehydrogenating primary and secondary alcohols, by ozonolysis of alkenes, and by hydration of alkynes. Rosenmund reduction turns an acyl chloride into an aldehyde; dialkyl cadmium turns it into a ketone. Nitriles give aldehydes by Stephen reduction or DIBAL-H, and ketones with Grignard reagents. Aromatic aldehydes come from Etard reaction, side chain chlorination of toluene, or Gatterman-Koch formylation; aromatic ketones from Friedel-Crafts acylation.","w":["Rosenmund's palladium is poisoned with BaSO₄ so the aldehyde is not reduced further to an alcohol."]},"12.5":{"s":"Carboxylic acids are made by hydrolysing nitriles and amides, acyl chlorides, anhydrides and esters (alkaline hydrolysis gives the salt, then acid frees the acid). Strong oxidation of an alkyl benzene gives benzoic acid whatever the length of the side chain. Alkenes are oxidised by acidified KMnO₄. A Grignard reagent added to dry ice (CO₂), then hydrolysed, gives an acid with one more carbon.","w":["Side chain oxidation needs a hydrogen on the carbon next to the ring; a tertiary alkyl group is not oxidised."]},"12.6":{"s":"The polar C=O group gives aldehydes and ketones dipole-dipole forces, so they boil higher than alkanes and ethers of similar mass but lower than alcohols, which hydrogen bond. Lower members dissolve in water by hydrogen bonding with it. Carboxylic acids boil highest of all because they form hydrogen-bonded dimers. Solubility in water falls as the hydrocarbon part grows.","w":["Boiling point order at similar mass: carboxylic acid > alcohol > ketone > aldehyde > ether > alkane."]},"12.7":{"s":"Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so the carbonyl carbon is slightly positive and is attacked by nucleophiles. Aldehydes react faster than ketones for two reasons: ketones have two electron-donating alkyl groups that reduce the positive charge, and two bulky groups that crowd the carbon. Aromatic aldehydes are less reactive than aliphatic ones because the ring donates electrons by resonance.","w":["Reactivity towards nucleophiles: HCHO > other aldehydes > ketones."]},"12.8":{"s":"Aldehydes are easily oxidised, so they give Tollens' (silver mirror), Fehling's (red Cu₂O) and Schiff's (pink) tests; ketones do not. Ketones give a red colour with sodium nitroprusside. Both add nucleophiles such as HCN, NaHSO₃, alcohols and Grignard reagents, and form imines with ammonia derivatives. Methyl ketones give the iodoform test. Aldehydes and ketones with an α-hydrogen undergo aldol condensation; aldehydes without one undergo Cannizzaro reaction. Clemmensen and Wolff-Kishner reductions turn C=O into CH₂.","w":["Benzaldehyde gives Tollens' test but not Fehling's test.","Aldol needs an α-hydrogen; Cannizzaro needs NO α-hydrogen (HCHO, C₆H₅CHO) and concentrated alkali."]},"12.9":{"s":"Carboxylic acids are acidic because the carboxylate ion left after losing H⁺ is stabilised by two equal resonance structures. Electron-withdrawing groups (halogens, NO₂) stabilise it further and make the acid stronger; electron-donating groups make it weaker. They react with metals and bases, give CO₂ with sodium bicarbonate, and form acyl chlorides, amides, anhydrides and esters. Soda lime removes CO₂ (decarboxylation); LiAlH₄ reduces them to primary alcohols.","w":["Lower pKa means a stronger acid: Cl₃CCOOH (0.6) is much stronger than CH₃COOH (4.76).","Acidity order: carboxylic acid > phenol > alcohol, because the carboxylate ion is best stabilised."]}};
